3. SecureMessageSource.java

This is the class that connects your existing ResourceBundleMessageSource to the decryption logic.

Instead of changing every existing routine’s code, you can centralize the decryption.

package com.new.example.security;
import java.security.GeneralSecurityException;
import org.springframework.context.support.ResourceBundleMessageSource;
/**
 * Extension of Spring ResourceBundleMessageSource
 * that transparently decrypts ENC(...) values.
 */
public class SecureMessageSource
        extends ResourceBundleMessageSource {
    private Aes256GcmEncryptionService encryptionService;
    public void setEncryptionService(
            Aes256GcmEncryptionService encryptionService) {
        this.encryptionService = encryptionService;
    }
    @Override
    protected String resolveCodeWithoutArguments(
            String code,
            java.util.Locale locale) {
        String value =
                super.resolveCodeWithoutArguments(
                        code,
                        locale);
        if (value == null) {
            return null;
        }
        if (!encryptionService.isEncrypted(value)) {
            return value;
        }
        try {
            return encryptionService.decrypt(value);
        } catch (GeneralSecurityException e) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                    "Unable to decrypt configuration value: "
                    + code,
                    e);
        }
    }
}

This is the important part for your existing architecture.

Your existing code can continue doing:

String username =
        messageSource.getMessage(
                "sftp.username",
                null,
                locale);
String password =
        messageSource.getMessage(
                "sftp.password",
                null,
                locale);

The application doesn’t need to explicitly call:

decrypt(password);

because SecureMessageSource handles it.

